# Public project identifier from the Reown dashboard.
VITE_WALLETCONNECT_PROJECT_ID=your_reown_project_id

# OPTIONAL: the Farcaster account THIS copy of Fiddy asks with, so a person can
# approve a posting key in their own phone app instead of typing a recovery phrase
# (docs/CLIENT-SELF-HOSTED-CONNECT.md). It is the instance's own account, not the
# person's: a FID, and that FID's custody key (0x… or twelve words), kept on this
# machine. Exporting both before `fiddy client up` is enough: the dev server it
# starts is its own child process (and this file is not where a custody key should
# be written down).
# Leave both unset and it still works: the project's own signer asks instead, and
# the approval is unchanged apart from whose name is on it.
# FIDDY_INSTANCE_APP_FID=3356372
# FIDDY_INSTANCE_APP_KEY=0x…
# OPTIONAL: which signer service to ask when there is no account of its own
# (default the line below; set it to an empty value to ask nobody).
# FIDDY_SIGNER_REQUEST_URL=https://fiddy.fun/api/fiddy/signer-request

# Optional browser-compatible Ethereum RPC override.
# VITE_ETHEREUM_RPC_URL=https://ethereum-rpc.publicnode.com

# Optional browser-compatible Arbitrum One RPC override.
# VITE_ARBITRUM_RPC_URL=https://arb1.arbitrum.io/rpc

# Optional browser-compatible BNB Smart Chain RPC override.
# VITE_BSC_RPC_URL=https://bsc-dataseed.bnbchain.org

# Optional browser-compatible Celo RPC override.
# VITE_CELO_RPC_URL=https://forno.celo.org

# Optional browser-compatible Monad RPC override.
# VITE_MONAD_RPC_URL=https://rpc.monad.xyz

# Optional browser-compatible HyperEVM RPC override.
# VITE_HYPEREVM_RPC_URL=https://rpc.hyperliquid.xyz/evm

# Optional browser-compatible Robinhood Chain RPC override.
# VITE_ROBINHOOD_RPC_URL=https://rpc.mainnet.chain.robinhood.com

# Optional browser-compatible Polygon RPC override. Polygon is not a dashboard
# network yet; the built-in default is 1rpc.io/matic because viem's bundled
# endpoint answers 403 to a keyless caller.
# VITE_POLYGON_RPC_URL=https://1rpc.io/matic

# Optional direct browser-side Solana RPC override. This URL is exposed in the
# built app. Prefer the server-side SOLANA_RPC_URL secret for hosted deployments.
# VITE_SOLANA_RPC_URL=https://your-solana-rpc.example

# Cloudflare Pages runtime secret used by the same-origin Solana read relay.
# Configure this in Cloudflare, not in a VITE_ variable or committed env file.
# SOLANA_RPC_URL=https://your-private-solana-rpc.example

# =============================================================================
# Signing up with Google or X (Privy)
# =============================================================================
# The way in offers three doors: Farcaster (this client's own sign-in), Google
# and X. The last two come from ONE Privy app, shared with the console in
# legitmate-farcaster — it exports the very same value as
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PRIVY_APP_ID. There is one Privy integration behind both surfaces;
# never add a second one.
#
# The id is public by design: it identifies the app to the browser SDK and it
# ships in the built bundle. PRIVY_APP_SECRET is the other half, it is a server
# secret, and nothing in this client reads it.
#
# Set it here for a dev server, and in the environment a release is built in
# (see docs/CLIENT-RELEASE.md). Unset — or empty — means the feature is absent
# rather than broken: no Google or X door is drawn, and Farcaster sign-in behaves
# exactly as it did before.
#
# Which of the doors a person can actually finish is a switch in the Privy
# dashboard (`disallowed_login_method`), never a line of code: with a method
# switched off, that door says so in a sentence and points at the other ways in.
#
# VITE_PRIVY_APP_ID=<the app id from the Privy dashboard>

# =============================================================================
# Snapchain Node Configuration
# =============================================================================
# The Fiddy web client can read feeds and submit casts through your own
# Snapchain read node instead of the Warpcast API. This requires a local node
# running at http://127.0.0.1:3381 (SSH tunnel default).
#
# Server-side proxy target: which URL the dev/preview server forwards to.
# Set this for production/Cloudflare deployments.
# SNAPCHAIN_HTTP_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3381
#
# Client-side direct URL (optional): use when the client is served from the
# same origin as the node (e.g., behind the same reverse proxy). Note: the node
# does not answer CORS preflight, so a direct call will fail for POST requests
# with application/octet-stream. Prefer the proxy path or SSH tunnel.
# VITE_SNAPCHAIN_HTTP_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3381
#
# To connect to a remote node via SSH tunnel:
#   ssh -N -L 3381:127.0.0.1:3381 user@your-node
# Then use the default http://127.0.0.1:3381 or set SNAPCHAIN_HTTP_URL.
#
# =============================================================================
# Which network that node speaks (Snapchain or Hypersnap)
# =============================================================================
# Two networks carry the same kind of social data and do not talk to each
# other. Snapchain is Farcaster's own network — the one every account, cast and
# follow lives on; pick this. Hypersnap is an independent parallel network built
# by the Quilibrium community — same kind of social data, different plumbing and
# different economics; it is for explorers, not where your Farcaster account
# lives.
#
# A node does NOT announce which network it is: MEASURED 2026-10-24, GET
# /v1/info answers the same field names on both, with no network name and a
# version string of shared lineage. So the client can never infer it — whoever
# points the client at a node records it here, and Settings → your node shows it
# (`fiddy client up` writes this line for you). Unset means "not recorded", and
# the client says exactly that instead of guessing.
#
# VITE_FIDDY_NETWORK=snapchain
# VITE_FIDDY_NETWORK=hypersnap

# =============================================================================
# Media host (photo and clip uploads)
# =============================================================================
# The composer uploads cast media to OUR own host, not to Warpcast's Cloudflare
# account: POST <base>/api/fiddy/media. The endpoint lives in the service repo
# (/Users/lc/legitmate-farcaster app/api/fiddy/media), and the adapter maps the
# fork's `/v1/generate-image-upload-url` to it. The URL that comes back from the
# upload is the URL the cast embeds.
#
# Unset means the production host: https://fiddy.fun
#
# Running the service locally beside the client:
#   cd /Users/lc/legitmate-farcaster
#   FIDDY_MEDIA_DIR=$HOME/.fiddy-media-dev LEGITMATE_SINGLE_USER_SUBJECT=dev-owner \
#   FIDDY_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://localhost:5174 FIDDY_MEDIA_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:3217 \
#   npx next dev -p 3217
#   cd /Users/lc/Projects/fiddy/apps/farcaster-web
#   VITE_FIDDY_MEDIA_URL=http://localhost:3217 VITE_SNAPCHAIN_DEBUG=true npx vite --port 5174
# Then: node scripts/verify-media-upload.mjs
VITE_FIDDY_MEDIA_URL=http://localhost:3217
